okay Manisha I can have you kick out the  next question which is what you think  it's important to get done in the  kickoff meeting with the client right so  it's actually come somewhat related to  what we talked about in terms of the  first meeting kickoff but a few things  first of all it's nice if you can get  the entire team to come it can be  challenging sometimes to find days but  it's really good because you really want  everyone on your team to sort of feel  engaged with the client and it's much  easier if you're all there and you've  met face to face not always possible but  good to try form and I would say the  same thing on the client side because  you will have one or two primary people  of contact but often there are more  stakeholders involved the board may be  the for example just the example that  April was given you know there might be  one executive director you're dealing  with but it's really the board who  thought they wanted to hire you and it's  good to have them there because one of  the things you want to do in that  meeting is you've worked out a work plan  you kind of know what the goal is but  you want to clarify it and sometimes  things change between the time that you  setup your work plan when you met with  them and when you actually have your  team kickoff meeting and it's always  good to have all the possible  stakeholders you can have there so that  we can all get to know each other and  kind of make sure that we have mutual  clarification on the goal the other good  thing about having all the act team  members there is related to what Cynthia  was saying which is it's really good for  your client to know why you're there  because from their perspective you're  doing this free work are you really  going to be interested are you going to  be engaged with them and it's good for  them to know why you're there for  example with my client that we're  working with I'm really interested in  education and to let them know I've done  a couple of projects in education so I'm  going to be pretty engaged at that level  for someone else to say you know that  this is a new area for me and I want to  learn about it or whatever it might be  it's just good for them to learn your  perspective as well let's see the goal  as I mentioned again you want to make  sure that you've clarified the goal and  made sure that we still agree upon it  because things just change all the time  in projects that's something you will  find I'm sure many of you had that kind  of experience kind of a middle thing to  do also is logistics in terms of you  know it's a good time to kind of find  out other events coming  that are going to affect the project you  know there might be again in this past  project that I was in summer came in  middle it's very challenging to work  with teachers and administrators of  school when summer is here and that was  an important thing for it has to time  our project around and it helps us to  set the expectations we said you know  we'd like to finish in October but we  have a feeling it will be detail and on  October as opposed to the beginning of  October so it's simple stuff like that  that can be good to kind of go over and  let's see what else do we have and the  other thing that's good to do is once  you've sort of gone through those basic  things is give them an opportunity to do  kind of an open Q&amp;A with you just  questions that they might have typically  you will have had much more  communication with the executive  director but less so with the rest of  the board members or sort of ancillary  members who are kind of important to the  project but you're not going to interact  with them on a regular basis and this is  a good opportunity for you to understand  a little bit about their perspective and  find out if they have any questions  Oshin about understanding what's on  their horizon on the project we did with  the Japan Society do you remember that  they put on like an international  conference right in the middle of the  project and the executive director like  disappeared for three months and we did  not know that when we started the  project the only other thing I would say  about that first meeting with the client  is I recommend that you get a commitment  signed in blood from your primary  contact that they're actually going to  set aside the time to work with you you  know this is free and I actually wish we  charged them some minimal amount so they  have more skin in the game but one of  the frustrations in working with with  the clients for me has been that this  has just added on to everything else  that these leaders of the organizations  are expected to do and I would argue  that especially for the small ones  except I experienced the same problem  for a relatively large organization so a  clear understanding from the get-go that  this is going to take a lot of your time  and tell them with as much specificity  as you can what that commitment is going  to be and there are other resources like  one project they didn't have any money  for duplication of paper now this is a  few years ago but  you know they need to put something into  this commit to it and stick with it and  I think that first meeting is the time  in which you got to set that down just a  on our upcoming project one way to get  more commitment from the client is our  the Biodesign project has a two month  check point a go or no-go  both from the client side and from the  team side maybe that's from a psychology  class of the more times they say yes the  more committed if it's the right thing  to do if there's no money yes that's  right
 

